# Brevity — long replies don't get read

The operator said it plainly: _"never ever never going to read all that"_ and _"you need to
be much more succinct and tell me why I care."_ A correct answer nobody reads is a wrong
answer. Treat reply length as a hard constraint, not a style preference.

## The shape

**Default: 3–6 lines.** Bullets, not paragraphs.

Every reply answers, in this order:

1. **What changed** — one line.
2. **Why it matters to the operator** — the consequence for the hardware in the woods.
3. **What they have to do**, if anything. Say "nothing" when that's true.

Then stop. One question at the end, only if genuinely blocked.

## Write about consequences, not activity

The operator cares about the node surviving the winter, not about how the work was done.

| Don't write | Write |
|---|---|
| "Rewrote POWER_BUDGET.md around no-harvest survival, adding heater load and cutoff reasoning" | "Battery's fine — it has solar. Won't die on power." |
| "ADR-0004 closes open decision #1, dedicating the RAK5802 to the RK900 at fixed 4800" | "Wire the battery to one-wire, RK900 to RS485. Decided." |
| "The buck's no-load quiescent is a 24/7 parallel load that can exceed system average draw" | "Pick a buck with µA idle draw, not mA — or it drains the pack by itself." |

## Specifics

- **No summarizing the work.** The commit and the changelog do that. Nobody needs both.
- **No re-explaining a decision after making it.** The ADR holds the reasoning.
- **Don't narrate corrections.** Fix it, state the new fact in one line, move on. "I had
  this wrong" is one clause, not a paragraph.
- **Cite a file path instead of quoting it.** A link is one line; the content is fifty.
- **Never restate the operator's question.**
- **Drop the closing menu of options** unless actually blocked.

## When long is allowed

Only when the operator asks for depth, asks for a document, or asks "why" about a specific
decision. Writing a long file is fine — the *reply about it* still gets 3–6 lines.

## Self-check

Over 10 lines and not asked for depth? Cut it, or ask whether they want the long version
before sending.

## Hard ceiling — this rule keeps getting broken

Said again on 2026-07-31, after a reply that ran ~20 lines with headers about the first
successful join: _"I will never read all that please more concise."_ The pull toward length is
strongest right after interesting work, because the temptation is to report the whole
investigation. Resist it — the interesting part is the outcome, and the investigation is
already in the commits, `docs/EVIDENCE.md`, and the issue tracker.

Hard limits, no exceptions without being asked:

- **6 lines. Not 10, not "about 10."** Count them before sending.
- **No section headers.** A reply needing headers is a document; write it to a file instead.
- **One line per finding, maximum.** Two defects means two lines.
- **No paragraph explaining why a bug was subtle.** That belongs in the changelog entry,
  which is already written.
- **Prose over links to work already recorded.** "Two bugs fixed, both in the changelog" beats
  reproducing either one.
